{"candidates":[{"claim":"RFC 9110 distinguishes safe from idempotent by side-effect permission: a safe method is intended only for retrieval and has no intended action beyond retrieval (section 9.2.1), while an idempotent method may have side effects but multiple identical requests have the same intended effect as one (section 9.2.2). An append-only log writer cannot be safe because appending changes durable state; it must be idempotent so that at-least-once redelivery of the same event yields the same log state as a single append.","counterconditions":["If duplicate appends are allowed to create distinct log entries and downstream replay is sensitive to multiplicity, this idempotent-writer claim fails.","If the transport already guarantees exactly-once delivery, idempotence is not exercised though it may still be defensive.","If the relevant protocol defines idempotence by identical responses rather than identical resulting server state, a retry returning 'already exists' instead of 'created' could violate the claim."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Key the event by a stable content/idempotency identifier and make the write a conditional append or upsert: if the identifier already exists, return without adding a new entry; if not, append exactly once. Thus the state transition for duplicate retries is S -> S union {e} after first and S -> S on later duplicates, equal to the single successful write.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"claim":"In formal terms, safety is the special case f(x)=no-op on server state; idempotence is f^n(x)=f(x). Therefore safe implies idempotent over intended state, but idempotent does not imply safe. The append-only log requires the writer property idempotence, not safety, because the write is a non-no-op side effect that must be stable under repetition.","counterconditions":["If the log's state domain counts duplicate entries as distinct, f_e is not idempotent.","If ordering or timestamps are part of state and duplicates alter order or timestamps, the equality f_e^n(S) = f_e(S) may not hold.","If safe is interpreted as 'safe to retry' rather than RFC 9110 safe, the implication safe => idempotent may not match the published definition."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Model the writer as f_e(S) = if e in S then S else S union {e}. One application mutates S; a second application leaves S unchanged; both lead to f_e(S), satisfying f_e(f_e(S)) = f_e(S). Safety would require f_e(S) = S for all S, which no genuine append can satisfy.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"claim":"An append-only log does not strictly require writers to be RFC-safe or RFC-idempotent; it requires deterministic replay invariants immune to duplicate delivery. If duplicate entries are persisted but the replay fold treats the log as a content-addressed set rather than a multiset, the writer can always append yet replay remains deterministic. The RFC terms are about HTTP method intended effects, not a generic durable log protocol.","counterconditions":["If replay treats multiplicity, ordering, or non-content timestamps as significant, absorption fails and duplicate entries diverge.","If the question insists on the writer boundary only rather than downstream replay, this shifts the required property to consumers not writers.","If the log must be a clean append-only stream without downstream dedup, duplicate entries violate determinism."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Let the log be a multiset W and replay state R = fold(W, deterministic reducer) where the reducer absorbs duplicate event ids, for example via set union. The writer appends every delivered copy, but the fold's absorption layer makes final replay state equal under duplicates.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.35},{"claim":"The precise distinction is scope of constraint: safe forbids intended side effects in a single call; idempotent permits side effects but requires repeated identical calls to converge to the same intended effect. For an append-only event log, readers/replayers must be safe (no mutation during replay) and writers must be idempotent; requiring writers to be safe would forbid the append itself.","counterconditions":["If replay mutates durable state as a side effect of folding, the readers-as-safe assumption fails and the distinction collapses."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Separate read and write paths: replay reads the log and folds state with no side effects, while a writer accepts at-least-once redelivery and deduplicates by id.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.0}],"simulation_proposals":[]}